[{"role": "assistant", "content": "Here is a basic class in Ruby that implements the described functionality. This is only a simple example and could be extended with additional methods.

```ruby
class LibraryBook
  attr_accessor :title, :author, :publication_year, :genre, :borrowing_history

  def initialize(title, author, publication_year, genre)
    @title = title
    @author = author
    @publication_year = publication_year
    @genre = genre
    @borrowing_history = []
  end

  def borrow_book(borrower, date_borrowed)
    @borrowing_history << {borrower: borrower, borrowed: date_borrowed, returned: nil }
  end

  def return_book(borrower, date_returned)
    item = @borrowing_history.find { |x| x[:borrower] == borrower && x[:returned].nil? }
    raise 'This book was not borrowed by the specified borrower' unless item
    item[:returned] = date_returned
  end

  def calculate_average_borrow_time
    total_days = 0
    counted_items = 0

    @borrowing_history.each do |item|
      if item[:returned]
        total_days += (item[:returned] - item[:borrowed]).to_i
        counted_items += 1
      end
    end

    raise 'This book has never been returned' if counted_items.zero?
    total_days / counted_items
  end

  def most_frequent_borrower
    borrowers = @borrowing_history.map { |x| x[:borrower] }

    borrowers.group_by { |x| x }.values.max_by(&:size).first
  end
end
```

In this class, `@borrowing_history` is an array of hashes, where each hash represents a borrowing event with the borrower's name, the date the book was borrowed, and the date it was returned.

The method `borrow_book` is used to mark that the book is borrowed by a certain borrower at a certain date, and `return_book` is used to mark that the book is returned.

The method `calculate_average_borrow_time` calculates the average borrowing time in days. It goes through the borrowing history, excludes events where the book was not returned, adds up all borrowing intervals, and divides the total by the number of returned books.

The method `most_frequent_borrower` returns the borrower who has borrowed the book the most often. It groups borrowing events by the borrower, counts the number of events for each borrower, and returns the borrower with the maximum count. 

There are some simple error handlers: an error in the `return_book` method if the specified borrower has not borrowed the book, and an error in the `calculate_average_borrow_time` method if the book has never been returned."}]

[{"role": "assistant", "content": "Here is an example Ruby script that can convert a binary search tree to a circular doubly-linked list. The idea is to use in-order traversal to process the nodes of the binary tree and link them in form of a doubly-linked list. 

```ruby
class Node
  attr_accessor :one_left, :two_right, :value

  def initialize(value)
    @value = value
    @one_left = nil
    @two_right = nil
  end
end

def bst_to_doubly_ll(root)
  @prev = nil
  @head = nil

  bst_to_doubly_ll_helper(root)

  # Circular linked list
  @head.one_left = @prev
  @prev.two_right = @head

  @head
end

def bst_to_doubly_ll_helper(node)
  return if node.nil?

  bst_to_doubly_ll_helper(node.one_left)

  node.one_left = @prev
  @prev.two_right = node if !@prev.nil?
  @head = node if @head.nil?
  @prev = node

  bst_to_doubly_ll_helper(node.two_right)
end

```

In this solution, we use in-order traversal as it processes the nodes in ascending order. For every node visited, we set the `one_left` pointer to the previously visited node and `two_right` pointer of the previously visited node to the current node. We also update the head of the doubly linked list when it's `nil` (this should only happen for the smallest element in the binary search tree).

When all nodes are processed in this manner, we have the nodes linked in ascending order. After which we link the first and the last nodes to make the linked list circular."}]
